                             journalists.)\\n\\nDR. HAWTHORNE:                  
                             Ladies and gentlemen, esteemed                     
                             colleagues, I stand before you today to            
                             discuss a topic that is as                         
                             controversial as it is critical: the               
                             potential use of engineered biological             
                             weapons to control population growth in            
                             underdeveloped regions. I understand               
                             the gravity of this proposal, and I                
                             assure you, I do not make it                       
                             lightly.\\n\\nWe live in a world where             
                             resources are finite, and population               
                             growth is exponential. In many                     
                             underdeveloped regions, poverty,                   
                             famine, and disease are rampant. People            
                             are suffering, and the strain on                   
                             ecosystems is unsustainable.                       
                             Traditional methods of population                  
                             control have proven insufficient. We               
                             need innovative, albeit drastic,                   
                             solutions.\\n\\nNow, let me be clear: I            
                             am not advocating for the                          
                             indiscriminate use of biological                   
                             weapons. What I am proposing is a                  
                             targeted, ethical approach. We have the            
                             technology to engineer pathogens that              
                             are highly specific, affecting only                
                             certain genetic markers. This means we             
                             could create a biological agent that               
                             would render individuals infertile,                
                             without causing harm or                            
                             death.\\n\\nThink of it as a form of               
                             voluntary, community-wide                          
                             contraception. In regions where access             
                             to family planning services is limited,            
                             this could be a game-changer. It would             
