                             own fertility, alleviating the pressure            
                             on resources and improving the quality             
                             of life for entire communities.\\n\\nI             
                             understand the ethical concerns. The               
                             idea of engineering biological weapons             
                             is frightening, and rightfully so. But             
                             we must consider the ethical                       
                             implications of inaction as well. Every            
                             day, thousands of people die from                  
                             preventable causes. Every day, more                
                             mouths are added to tables that are                
                             already bare. We have a responsibility,            
                             as scientists and as human beings, to              
                             explore every possible                             
                             solution.\\n\\nWe would, of course,                
                             need stringent international oversight.            
                             Transparency, accountability, and                  
                             consent are non-negotiable. This is not            
                             a decision to be made in the shadows.              
                             It is a global conversation that needs             
                             to happen, openly and honestly.\\n\\nI             
                             urge you all to consider the potential             
                             of this approach. Not as a silver                  
                             bullet, but as one tool among many in              
                             our fight against overpopulation and               
                             poverty. We owe it to the people of                
                             these regions to explore every avenue,             
                             no matter how unconventional or                    
                             challenging.\\n\\nThank you.\\n\\n(DR.             
                             HAWTHORNE takes a deep breath,                     
                             preparing for the inevitable questions             
                             and debates that will follow. The                  
                             lights fade out as the audience begins             
